insert into public.glossary (term, short_def, long_def, example, category, related_terms) values
  ('TAM', 'Total Addressable Market — the total yearly revenue you would earn if every possible customer in the world bought your product.', 'TAM answers ''how big is the prize if you won everything?''. It is estimated top-down (industry reports: ''Indian edtech = $10B'') or, more credibly, bottom-up (number of potential customers × what each would pay per year). VCs use TAM to judge whether a startup can ever get big enough to matter — a great team in a tiny market still builds a small company.', 'For a UPI-payments app, TAM is roughly all 350M+ Indians making digital payments × the revenue you could earn per user per year — not all 1.4 billion Indians.', 'market_sizing', '{"SAM","SOM"}'),
  ('SAM', 'Serviceable Addressable Market — the slice of TAM your product, language, geography and business model can actually serve today.', 'SAM narrows TAM to customers your current product can realistically reach — filtered by geography, language, price point and distribution. It is calculated bottom-up: customers who fit your constraints × annual revenue per customer. Investors read SAM as ''the market you are actually competing in right now''.', 'An app teaching JEE prep only in Hindi has a SAM of Hindi-medium JEE aspirants — a few lakh students — not India''s entire 250M+ school-going population.', 'market_sizing', '{"TAM","SOM"}'),
  ('SOM', 'Serviceable Obtainable Market — the realistic chunk of SAM you can win in the next few years, given competition and your resources.', 'SOM is your honest near-term target: of the market you can serve (SAM), how much can you actually capture given competitors, budget and team? It is often estimated as a defensible market share (say 2-5% of SAM) backed by current traction and a distribution plan. Founders who claim ''we will take 10% of a $100B market'' without a path lose credibility instantly.', 'Zepto''s early SOM was not ''all Indian grocery'' — it was grocery orders from the handful of Mumbai and Bengaluru pincodes its first dark stores could deliver to in 10 minutes.', 'market_sizing', '{"TAM","SAM","Traction"}'),
  ('Moat', 'A durable advantage that keeps competitors from copying you — brand, network effects, switching costs, proprietary tech or data.', 'Borrowed from Warren Buffett (castles have moats), a moat is whatever keeps your lead defensible after rivals notice you. Common moats: network effects (the product improves as users join), high switching costs, economies of scale, brand trust, exclusive data or licenses. VCs probe for a moat because in India especially, a good idea gets cloned within months — features are not moats.', 'Naukri.com''s moat is a two-sided network effect: decades of recruiters and resumes in one place means both sides must stay; a new job portal cannot recreate that overnight.', 'product', '{"Product-Market Fit","Unit Economics"}'),
  ('Burn Rate', 'How much cash your startup loses per month. Net burn = monthly expenses minus revenue — the speed at which your bank balance shrinks.', 'Gross burn is total monthly spend (salaries, cloud, rent, marketing); net burn subtracts revenue and is the number that matters. It is tracked from your bank statement, not your projections. Burn is judged relative to progress: burning ₹1 crore a month is fine if revenue is compounding, fatal if only downloads are.', 'If your startup spends ₹12 lakh a month on salaries, AWS and ads while earning ₹4 lakh, your net burn is ₹8 lakh per month — Dunzo''s unchecked burn is a big reason it shut down in early 2025.', 'metrics', '{"Runway","Unit Economics"}'),
  ('Runway', 'Months of survival left: cash in bank divided by monthly net burn. At zero you shut down, raise again, or reach profitability.', 'Runway converts your bank balance into time: ₹80 lakh in the bank with ₹8 lakh monthly net burn = 10 months of runway. Since an Indian fundraise typically takes 3-6 months of meetings, diligence and paperwork, wise founders start raising with 9-12 months left. VCs ask about runway partly to gauge how desperate you are — never negotiate with 2 months remaining.', 'During the 2022-23 funding winter, Indian startups cut teams and marketing to stretch 12 months of runway into 24 while waiting for the market to thaw.', 'metrics', '{"Burn Rate","Bridge Round"}'),
  ('Dilution', 'The drop in your ownership percentage when new shares are issued to investors or ESOPs. A smaller slice of a (hopefully) bigger pie.', 'When a startup raises money it creates new shares for the investor, so every existing holder''s percentage falls even though their share count does not. Dilution is judged as a trade: giving up 15-25% per early round is normal if the capital multiplies company value. Careless founders who over-dilute early can own under 20% by Series C, losing both wealth and control.', 'You and your co-founder each own 50%. Your ₹4 crore seed round sells 20% in new shares — you both drop to 40%, but of a company now priced at ₹20 crore instead of a dream.', 'legal_equity', '{"Equity","Cap Table","Down Round"}'),
  ('Cap Table', 'Capitalization table — the master record of who owns what: every founder, investor and ESOP holder with their shares and percentages.', 'The cap table lists all shareholders, their share counts, share classes and ownership percentages, updated after every round, ESOP grant or transfer. Investors read it during due diligence to check founders still hold enough to stay motivated (healthy: founders 50%+ at seed) and that no dead weight owns a big chunk. A messy cap table — 30 tiny angels, an ex-cofounder squatting on 25% — can kill a deal by itself.', 'A typical Indian post-seed cap table: two founders 70%, ESOP pool 10%, angels 5%, seed VC 15% — tracked on platforms like Qapita or Hissa rather than Excel.', 'legal_equity', '{"Equity","Dilution","ESOP","Vesting & Cliff"}'),
  ('SAFE', 'Simple Agreement for Future Equity — investor pays now, gets shares at the next priced round. Fast, with no valuation debate today.', 'Invented by Y Combinator, a SAFE lets early investors put in money without pricing the company yet; the cash converts into shares at the next big round, usually with a valuation cap and/or discount rewarding early risk. It is not debt — no interest, no repayment date. The US-style SAFE does not fit India''s Companies Act cleanly, which is why the iSAFE variant exists; classic SAFEs appear mostly when Indian founders incorporate abroad.', 'An Indian founder joining Y Combinator typically flips to a US entity and raises YC''s standard $500K on SAFEs; back home, domestic rounds use iSAFE or CCPS instead.', 'fundraising', '{"iSAFE","Convertible Note","Pre-Money & Post-Money Valuation"}'),
  ('iSAFE', 'India SAFE — 100X.VC''s desi adaptation of the SAFE, issued as compulsorily convertible preference shares to satisfy Indian law.', 'US SAFEs are not recognised under India''s Companies Act, so in 2019 the VC firm 100X.VC created iSAFE: legally it is compulsorily convertible preference shares (CCPS) that behave like a SAFE — money now, automatic conversion to equity at the next priced round or a defined event, with a valuation cap or discount. Founders get speed (a few pages, no valuation negotiation) while staying compliant with Indian law. It has become a common instrument for Indian pre-seed and angel rounds.', '100X.VC invests about ₹1.25 crore in each startup of its class via iSAFE notes, then showcases them to hundreds of VCs at its Pitch Day.', 'fundraising', '{"SAFE","Convertible Note","Pre-Seed","Demo Day"}'),
  ('Convertible Note', 'A loan that converts into equity at the next round instead of being repaid — with interest, a maturity date, and a cap or discount.', 'The investor legally lends money today; at the next priced round the loan (plus interest) converts into shares, typically at a discount or under a valuation cap. Unlike a SAFE it is debt — interest and a maturity date give investors more protection. In India, only DPIIT-recognised startups may issue convertible notes, with a minimum of ₹25 lakh per investor in one tranche, convertible or repayable within 10 years.', 'A Bengaluru fintech with DPIIT recognition raises ₹1 crore from two angels as convertible notes; when its ₹8 crore seed round closes a year later, the notes convert into shares at a 20% discount.', 'fundraising', '{"SAFE","iSAFE","DPIIT Recognition","Seed Round"}'),
  ('Term Sheet', 'An investor''s offer summary: valuation, amount, board seats, liquidation preference and key rights. Mostly non-binding until signing.', 'A term sheet summarises the deal an investor proposes — how much, at what valuation, with what rights (board seat, liquidation preference, pro-rata, anti-dilution) — before lawyers draft the binding shareholders'' agreement. Most clauses are non-binding except exclusivity and confidentiality, and the deal can still die in due diligence. Founders are judged on reading beyond the valuation: a high price with brutal terms can be worse than a lower, cleaner offer.', 'The ''₹1 crore for 10%'' handshake on Shark Tank India is effectively a verbal term sheet — money moves only after diligence, which is why several aired deals never actually close.', 'fundraising', '{"Due Diligence","Liquidation Preference","Pro-Rata Rights","Pre-Money & Post-Money Valuation"}'),
  ('Pre-Money & Post-Money Valuation', 'Pre-money = company value before the new cash; post-money = pre-money + investment. Investor stake = investment ÷ post-money.', 'Valuation is what the deal says your company is worth. Pre-money is the value agreed before the investor''s cheque; add the cheque and you get post-money, which sets the investor''s ownership: invest ₹2 crore at ₹8 crore pre-money → ₹10 crore post-money → 20% stake. Early-stage valuation is not a spreadsheet truth — it is negotiated from traction, team, market size and comparable deals. Always clarify which number is being quoted; the difference changes your dilution.', 'A Shark offering ₹1 crore for 10% is pricing the startup at ₹10 crore post-money (₹9 crore pre-money) — when a Shark says ''that values you at...'', this is the math running.', 'fundraising', '{"Dilution","Term Sheet","Down Round"}'),
  ('Equity', 'Ownership in a company, held as shares. Its value is your percentage times company value — realised only when someone buys or you IPO.', 'Equity means owning a percentage of the company through shares; its value is your stake × company valuation, realised only at a liquidity event (acquisition, IPO, buyback). Founders trade equity for capital (investors) and talent (ESOPs). Judging equity means watching both the percentage and what it is a percentage of — 5% of a unicorn beats 50% of nothing.', 'Flipkart''s early employees'' small equity stakes became life-changing money when Walmart bought the company for $16 billion in 2018.', 'legal_equity', '{"ESOP","Dilution","Cap Table"}'),
  ('ESOP', 'Employee Stock Option Plan — options letting employees buy shares later at a fixed low price, so they gain as the startup grows.', 'An ESOP grants employees the option (not obligation) to buy shares at a preset ''exercise price'' after they vest over time; if the company''s share value rises far above that price, the difference is the employee''s gain. Startups use ESOPs to compete with big-company salaries. Judge an offer by the number of options, exercise price, vesting terms, and whether the company actually runs buybacks — and note India taxes gains once at exercise and again at sale.', 'Razorpay and Zerodha have run ESOP buybacks where employees sold vested options for cash; Flipkart''s Walmart deal unlocked roughly $500M for employees.', 'legal_equity', '{"Equity","Vesting & Cliff","Cap Table"}'),
  ('Vesting & Cliff', 'Vesting: you earn your equity gradually, typically over 4 years. Cliff: leave before year one and you walk away with nothing.', 'Vesting spreads equity ownership over time so people earn it by staying — the Indian and global standard is 4 years. The cliff is a probation period: quit (or get fired) before it ends, usually 12 months, and zero equity has vested; the moment you cross it, the first year''s chunk vests at once, then the rest monthly or quarterly. It protects startups from someone leaving in month 3 with a big permanent stake — this applies to founders too, and VCs will insist founder shares vest.', 'Join a Bengaluru startup with 4,800 ESOP options on 4-year vesting with a 1-year cliff: zero options for 12 months, then 1,200 vest at once, then 100 per month for 3 more years.', 'legal_equity', '{"ESOP","Equity","Cap Table"}'),
  ('Pre-Seed', 'The first outside money — usually ₹25 lakh to ₹2 crore in India — raised on the team, a deck and maybe a rough prototype.', 'Pre-seed is the earliest round, coming after (or alongside) friends-and-family money and before real traction exists. Cheques come from angels, micro VCs and pre-seed funds, usually via iSAFE or CCPS since pricing a company this early is guesswork. Investors here judge almost entirely on founders: their insight into the problem, speed of execution, and founder-market fit.', 'Two IIT Delhi final-years raising ₹60 lakh from Titan Capital and a couple of operator-angels to build v1 of their kirana-inventory SaaS — that is a classic Indian pre-seed.', 'fundraising', '{"Seed Round","Angel Investor","Micro VC","iSAFE","Founder-Market Fit"}'),
  ('Seed Round', 'The round that funds the search for product-market fit — in India typically ₹2-15 crore from seed VCs, micro VCs and angels.', 'Seed money pays for 12-24 months of building, launching and iterating until real users prove demand. Indian seed rounds are led by funds like Blume Ventures, India Quotient or 100X.VC, sized roughly ₹2-15 crore, either priced (shares for equity) or via iSAFE. What gets judged: early usage or revenue signals, a sharp wedge into a big market, and whether this team can find PMF before the money runs out.', 'Zepto''s teenage founders raised seed funding in 2021 to test whether Mumbai households would reorder groceries delivered in 10 minutes — that repeat-order data unlocked every later round.', 'fundraising', '{"Pre-Seed","Series A","Product-Market Fit","iSAFE"}'),
  ('Series A', 'The first large institutional round (₹30-120+ crore in India), raised once seed-stage metrics prove the model deserves scaling.', 'Series A is where storytelling stops being enough: funds like Peak XV, Accel or Elevation want evidence — meaningful revenue (often around $1M / ₹8 crore ARR for SaaS), strong retention, sane unit economics. It is a priced round: fresh preference shares, a proper valuation, usually a board seat for the lead investor. The bar is high — most seed-funded Indian startups never raise an A, a gap founders call the ''Series A crunch''.', 'A SaaS startup that grew from ₹1 crore to ₹8 crore ARR in 18 months with strong retention raising ₹60 crore led by Accel — that is a textbook Indian Series A.', 'fundraising', '{"Seed Round","Traction","Unit Economics","Term Sheet"}'),
  ('Bridge Round', 'A small in-between round — often from existing investors via convertibles — to extend runway until you hit the next round''s metrics.', 'A bridge ''bridges'' the gap when you are not yet ready for the next big round but runway is shrinking. Existing investors usually lead it using iSAFE or convertible notes to avoid setting a fresh valuation. It is judged by intent: a bridge to a clear milestone (''₹2 crore more ARR unlocks Series A'') is smart; a ''bridge to nowhere'' that just delays death is a red flag investors smell instantly.', 'During India''s 2022-23 funding winter, hundreds of startups raised ₹1-5 crore bridge rounds from insiders to survive until Series A appetite returned.', 'fundraising', '{"Runway","Convertible Note","Down Round","Seed Round"}'),
  ('Down Round', 'Raising at a lower valuation than your last round. Painful: heavy dilution, morale hit, and anti-dilution clauses may trigger.', 'A down round means the market now values you below your last price — often because the earlier valuation was inflated or growth stalled. It dilutes founders and employees hard (more shares issued per rupee), can trigger anti-dilution protection that mints extra shares for old investors, and crushes ESOP value. Still, it is judged better than dying: survive, rebuild, and the label fades.', 'PharmEasy, once valued at $5.6B, raised money in 2023 at roughly 90% lower — India''s most-cited down round; Byju''s fall from its $22B peak made the term dinner-table vocabulary.', 'fundraising', '{"Pre-Money & Post-Money Valuation","Dilution","Bridge Round"}'),
  ('Unicorn & Soonicorn', 'Unicorn: a private startup valued at $1B+ (~₹8,500 crore). Soonicorn: one in the hundreds of millions, expected to get there soon.', 'Coined in 2013 when billion-dollar startups were as rare as unicorns; India now has 110+ (Flipkart, Razorpay, Zepto and more), so the mythical vibe has faded. The label reflects the last round''s negotiated valuation, not profits — several Indian unicorns lose crores yearly, while bootstrapped Zerodha became one without raising VC money at all. ''Soonicorn'' is Indian startup-media slang for companies tipped to cross the $1B line next.', 'Zepto went from a Mumbai dark-store experiment (2021) to unicorn (2023); Indian business dailies publish annual ''soonicorn'' lists tracking who is next.', 'vc_speak', '{"Pre-Money & Post-Money Valuation","Down Round"}'),
  ('CAC', 'Customer Acquisition Cost — total sales and marketing spend divided by new customers gained. What you pay to ''buy'' one customer.', 'CAC = (ads + sales salaries + agency fees + acquisition discounts) ÷ customers gained in the period. Blended CAC averages across free and paid channels; paid CAC isolates the paid ones — investors ask for both. It is judged against LTV and payback period: an ₹800 CAC recovered in 3 months is great; recovered in 3 years, it is a slow leak.', 'If your edtech spends ₹5 lakh a month on Instagram ads and telecallers and signs up 100 paying students, CAC = ₹5,000 — you must earn more than that per student for the model to work.', 'metrics', '{"LTV","LTV:CAC Ratio","Unit Economics"}'),
  ('LTV', 'Lifetime Value — total profit one customer generates before leaving. Roughly: monthly margin per customer × months they stay.', 'LTV estimates what a customer is worth over their whole relationship with you. Quick formula: average revenue per user per month × gross margin % × average lifespan in months (lifespan ≈ 1 ÷ monthly churn). It is judged against CAC — spending ₹5,000 to acquire someone worth ₹3,000 is charity, not business. The classic beginner mistake is using revenue instead of margin, which flatters LTV badly.', 'A ₹299-per-month OTT subscriber at 80% gross margin who stays 15 months has an LTV of about ₹3,600 — that caps what you can sanely spend to acquire them.', 'metrics', '{"CAC","LTV:CAC Ratio","Churn","Gross Margin & Contribution Margin"}'),
  ('LTV:CAC Ratio', 'Lifetime value divided by acquisition cost. The classic healthy benchmark is 3:1 — each customer returns 3x what you paid to win them.', 'This ratio tells you whether your growth engine creates or destroys value: below 1:1 you lose money on every customer; around 3:1 is the celebrated benchmark; far above 5:1 may mean you are under-investing in growth. It must be paired with CAC payback time — a 4:1 ratio that takes 4 years to collect can still kill a cash-poor startup. Early-stage numbers are noisy, so VCs read the trend more than the snapshot.', 'CAC of ₹5,000 against LTV of ₹6,000 is 1.2:1 — an Indian D2C brand at that ratio is quietly burning money with every ''growth'' campaign it celebrates.', 'metrics', '{"LTV","CAC","Unit Economics"}'),
  ('Churn', 'The percentage of customers (or revenue) you lose in a period. The silent killer: high churn means you are filling a leaking bucket.', 'Customer churn = customers lost in a month ÷ customers at the start of it; revenue churn weights by money, which matters more. Even ''small'' monthly churn compounds brutally: 5% monthly means losing about 46% of your customers within a year. Retention (churn''s inverse) is the strongest single evidence of product-market fit, and good investors study churn cohorts before they look at growth.', 'An Indian SaaS starting the month with 200 customers and losing 10 has 5% monthly churn — its sales team must replace nearly half the base every year just to stay flat.', 'metrics', '{"LTV","MRR & ARR","Product-Market Fit"}'),
  ('MRR & ARR', 'Monthly / Annual Recurring Revenue — predictable subscription revenue. ARR = MRR × 12. The heartbeat metrics of SaaS startups.', 'MRR counts only recurring subscription revenue normalised per month (one-time setup fees and one-off projects do not count); ARR is MRR × 12, used for annual contracts and headline sizing. Investors track its composition: new MRR, expansion MRR (upgrades) and churned MRR. Indian SaaS milestones are spoken in ARR — ''$1M ARR'' (about ₹8.5 crore) is the classic seed-to-Series-A rite of passage.', '1,000 customers paying ₹4,000 per month = ₹40 lakh MRR = ₹4.8 crore ARR; Zoho and Freshworks built billion-dollar businesses measured this way.', 'metrics', '{"Churn","Series A","Traction"}'),
  ('GMV', 'Gross Merchandise Value — the total value of goods sold through your platform. Impressive, but it is not your revenue.', 'GMV sums the full price of everything transacted on a marketplace in a period. The platform''s actual revenue is only its cut — commission, fees, ads (see take rate). GMV is judged with suspicion: it is easily inflated by discounts and returns, so investors ask for net GMV (after returns and cancellations) and, more importantly, the revenue and contribution margin hiding underneath.', 'If Flipkart''s Big Billion Days sells ₹50,000 crore of phones and fashion, that is GMV; Flipkart''s revenue is only its slice of commissions, fees and logistics charges.', 'metrics', '{"Take Rate","MRR & ARR","Vanity Metrics"}'),
  ('Take Rate', 'The percentage of GMV a marketplace keeps as its own revenue — commissions plus fees, divided by total transaction value.', 'Take rate = platform revenue ÷ GMV. It captures how much value the marketplace extracts per transaction and differs wildly by category: payments ~0.1-2%, e-commerce ~10-20%, food delivery ~20-30%. It is judged as a tension dial — push it too high and sellers revolt or leave; too low and you cannot cover costs. A rising take rate with stable seller retention is the dream.', 'On a ₹500 Zomato order, the restaurant may pay ₹100-125 in commission and fees — a 20-25% take rate; UPI''s zero MDR is why pure UPI apps earn almost nothing per payment.', 'metrics', '{"GMV","Unit Economics","B2B/B2C/B2B2C"}'),
  ('Unit Economics', 'Profit or loss on one unit of your business (an order, a user, a seat) — proof the machine works before you pour fuel on it.', 'Unit economics zoom into a single unit — an order, a ride, a subscriber — and total all directly attributable revenue and costs to see if you make money on it. Core tools: contribution margin per unit, and CAC versus LTV. The judgment rule: scaling with negative unit economics just manufactures bigger losses — ''we lose money on every sale but make it up in volume'' is the oldest joke in venture capital.', 'A ₹400 quick-commerce order: goods margin ₹60, minus rider ₹35, dark store ₹20, packaging ₹5, discount ₹15 — a ₹15 loss per order; multiply by a million orders and you see why 10-minute delivery burned crores.', 'metrics', '{"Gross Margin & Contribution Margin","CAC","LTV","Burn Rate"}'),
  ('Gross Margin & Contribution Margin', 'Gross margin: revenue minus direct product cost. Contribution margin: also subtract variable costs like delivery, fees and packaging.', 'Gross margin = (revenue − cost of goods sold) ÷ revenue: what remains after making or procuring the thing (software 80%+, D2C 50-65%, groceries 15-20%). Contribution margin goes further, subtracting all variable costs per sale — logistics, payment gateway, marketplace commission, packaging — showing what each sale truly ''contributes'' toward fixed costs like salaries. Investors check that contribution is positive and improving; if it is negative, every extra sale digs the hole deeper.', 'A boAt-style earbud sells at ₹2,000 with ₹1,200 landed cost — 40% gross margin; after ₹300 of marketplace commission and shipping, contribution margin is ₹500, i.e. 25%.', 'metrics', '{"Unit Economics","Burn Rate","Take Rate"}'),
  ('Product-Market Fit', 'The stage where your product satisfies real market demand — users stick, refer others, and growth starts pulling instead of pushing.', 'PMF means you have built something a specific market genuinely wants: usage retains without bribes, word-of-mouth brings users, and demand strains your capacity. It is judged through retention cohorts that flatten (people keep coming back), the share of organic growth, and proxies like the Sean Ellis test (40%+ of users saying they would be ''very disappointed'' without you). Before PMF, a startup''s only real job is finding it — scaling marketing before PMF is pouring water into a cracked pot.', 'Early Zerodha barely advertised; traders told other traders. When your Bengaluru beta users WhatsApp you at 6 am asking why the app is down, you are smelling PMF.', 'product', '{"MVP","Traction","Churn","Pivot"}'),
  ('MVP', 'Minimum Viable Product — the smallest, fastest version that tests your core assumption with real users before you build the full thing.', 'An MVP strips the idea down to its riskiest assumption and tests it cheaply: a landing page, a WhatsApp group, a no-code app, even founders doing the service manually (a ''concierge MVP''). Success is measured in validated learning — did real users behave as predicted (signups, payments, repeat usage)? The classic beginner mistake is polishing for 8 months; the point is maximum learning per week, not a miniature final product.', 'Many Indian D2C brands ''launch'' as an Instagram page plus Google Form plus UPI QR code; Dunzo famously began as founders fulfilling errands over a WhatsApp group.', 'product', '{"Product-Market Fit","Pivot","Traction"}'),
  ('Pivot', 'A deliberate change in core strategy — new customer, product or business model — while keeping the team and what you have learned.', 'A pivot is not panic-quitting; it is redirecting the company after evidence shows the current path will not reach product-market fit. Good pivots keep an asset — the team, technology, audience or hard-won insight — and change one big variable: customer segment, problem, channel or revenue model. It is judged by honesty and speed: recognising the dead end early beats burning two more years of runway on pride.', 'Meesho began as FashNear, a hyperlocal fashion-delivery app; it pivoted to powering reseller entrepreneurs on WhatsApp and became a multi-billion-dollar social commerce company.', 'product', '{"Product-Market Fit","MVP","Runway"}'),
  ('GTM', 'Go-To-Market — your concrete plan for reaching customers and earning money: target segment, channels, pricing and sales motion.', 'GTM answers: who exactly do we sell to first, through which channels (content, ads, field sales, partnerships, app stores), at what price, with what pitch? A good GTM is sequenced — win a beachhead segment, then expand — because ''everyone'' is not a strategy. Investors judge GTM by per-channel economics (CAC per channel) and whether the motion matches the price point: you cannot afford field sales for a ₹99-per-month app.', 'Jio''s GTM was brutal and brilliant: free 4G for months to acquire 100M+ users in 170 days, then convert them to paid plans once the habit had formed.', 'gtm', '{"ICP","PLG","CAC","B2B/B2C/B2B2C"}'),
  ('ICP', 'Ideal Customer Profile — a precise description of the customer who needs you most, buys fastest, pays well, and sticks around.', 'Your ICP defines the exact segment to focus on: firmographics (industry, company size) for B2B or demographics and behaviour for B2C, plus the trigger that makes them buy. It is derived from your best existing users — who retains, expands and refers — not from imagination. A sharp ICP concentrates limited money on winnable customers; a vague one (''all SMEs'', ''all students'') guarantees wasted CAC.', 'Instead of ''Indian shopkeepers'', a sharp ICP reads: ''single-outlet pharmacies in tier-2 cities doing ₹3-10 lakh monthly sales, whose owner already takes orders on WhatsApp''.', 'gtm', '{"GTM","Product-Market Fit","CAC"}'),
  ('B2B/B2C/B2B2C', 'Who you sell to: B2B = to businesses, B2C = to consumers, B2B2C = through a business partner to reach its consumers.', 'B2B startups sell to companies — fewer, bigger, slower deals won by sales teams. B2C sells directly to individuals — huge volumes, small tickets, won by marketing and retention. B2B2C partners with a business that carries your product to its end-consumers, sharing the economics. The model dictates everything downstream — sales cycle, CAC, pricing, even the fundraising narrative — which is why VCs ask it first.', 'Zoho selling software to companies is B2B; Zomato feeding individuals is B2C; a fintech powering ''insurance inside Paytm'' rides Paytm''s user base — B2B2C.', 'gtm', '{"GTM","ICP","PLG"}'),
  ('PLG', 'Product-Led Growth — the product itself acquires and converts users via free tiers, self-serve and virality, before sales calls anyone.', 'In PLG, users discover a free or cheap version, onboard themselves, get value, invite teammates, and upgrade — sales enters late, if at all, to close big accounts. It suits products with fast time-to-value and natural sharing loops, and shows up in metrics like free-to-paid conversion and expansion revenue. The opposite motion is sales-led growth; many Indian SaaS companies blend both as they move upmarket.', 'Postman, built from Bengaluru, spread developer-to-developer as a free API tool long before it had a sales team — it reached a $5.6B valuation on that PLG engine.', 'gtm', '{"GTM","Product-Market Fit","MRR & ARR"}'),
  ('Founder-Market Fit', 'How uniquely suited this founder is to this market — lived experience, domain insight, or networks that rivals cannot easily match.', 'Before traction exists, investors bet on people, and founder-market fit asks: why are YOU the one to win this market? Evidence includes years inside the industry, having personally suffered the problem, rare technical skill, or distribution the market already trusts. It is judged in the first ten minutes of a pitch — a founder who knows where the market''s bodies are buried beats a smart outsider with slides.', 'Nithin Kamath traded the markets for a decade before founding Zerodha; Alakh Pandey taught the exact students Physics Wallah now serves — VCs call this founder-market fit.', 'vc_speak', '{"Product-Market Fit","Pre-Seed","Traction"}'),
  ('Vitamin vs Painkiller', 'Painkiller: solves an urgent, burning problem people pay for now. Vitamin: a nice-to-have that gets cut first. VCs want painkillers.', 'The classic investor filter: does your product remove acute pain (a painkiller) or merely add a mild benefit (a vitamin)? Painkillers show up as urgent budgets, fast sales cycles and low churn; vitamins get ''interesting, maybe later'' and die in downturns. Test it by behaviour, not compliments: do users pay quickly, come back, and complain loudly when it breaks? Some vitamins (like social apps) become habit-forming painkillers — but do not bet your pitch on that.', 'A resume-font beautifier is a vitamin; instant working-capital credit for an MSME whose buyers pay 90 days late — the pain OfBusiness and Khatabook chase — is a painkiller.', 'product', '{"Product-Market Fit","ICP","Moat"}'),
  ('Traction', 'Concrete evidence that your startup is working: paying users, revenue, retention, growth rate. Numbers that de-risk your story.', 'Traction is proof of demand expressed in metrics that move — revenue or MRR, active users, repeat and retention rates, signed contracts, waitlists that convert. It is judged by trajectory and quality, not size alone: ₹2 lakh MRR growing 30% month-on-month with strong retention beats ₹20 lakh flat. Each funding stage expects deeper traction, and the traction slide is usually the most-read slide in an Indian pitch deck.', '''2,000 paying users, 30% month-on-month growth, 85% month-3 retention'' is traction; ''50,000 Instagram followers and an IIT alumni WhatsApp group that loves the idea'' is not.', 'metrics', '{"Vanity Metrics","Product-Market Fit","MRR & ARR","Series A"}'),
  ('Vanity Metrics', 'Numbers that look impressive but prove no business health — downloads, followers, registered users, GMV puffed up by discounts.', 'Vanity metrics go up and to the right while revealing nothing about real value creation: total downloads, cumulative signups, page views, app-store ranks. The tell: they can never go down, are not tied to revenue or retention, and do not inform decisions. The cure is actionable metrics — activation, retention, paying users, contribution margin. Indian edtech''s boom-and-bust made investors allergic to ''lakhs of learners'' with no paying, returning ones.', '''10 lakh downloads'' impresses relatives; a VC will ask how many opened the app this week (maybe 3%) and how many paid (maybe 0.4%).', 'metrics', '{"Traction","Churn","GMV"}'),
  ('Due Diligence', 'The investor''s background check before money moves — verifying financials, legal records, tech, team, and every claim in your pitch.', 'After a term sheet, investors and their lawyers and accountants verify everything: GST filings and bank statements versus claimed revenue, cap table cleanliness, contracts, compliance, code quality, founder references, even customer calls. In India expect 4-8+ weeks. It runs both ways — smart founders also diligence investors by calling their portfolio founders. Deals die here when reality does not match the pitch, so never inflate numbers you will have to prove.', 'Several Shark Tank India deals shown on TV never closed because due diligence found the revenue claimed on air did not match GST filings and bank statements.', 'fundraising', '{"Term Sheet","Cap Table","Traction"}'),
  ('Deal Flow', 'The stream of investment opportunities a VC sees — decks, intros, referrals. Better deal flow means better companies to choose from.', 'Deal flow is the pipeline of startups reaching an investor: cold emails, founder referrals, demo days, scout networks, campus programs. VCs obsess over its quality because returns follow access — the best firms see the best companies first, often before a round officially opens. The founder lesson is practical: a warm intro from a founder the VC trusts enters high-quality deal flow; a cold LinkedIn DM usually does not.', 'A Bengaluru seed fund might screen 300 startups a month — sourced from IIT/IIM networks, 100X.VC pitch days and portfolio-founder referrals — and cut just 2 cheques.', 'vc_speak', '{"Demo Day","Micro VC","Dry Powder"}'),
  ('Dry Powder', 'Money a VC fund has raised from its backers but not yet invested — capital sitting ready to be deployed into startups.', 'VC funds raise a corpus from Limited Partners (pension funds, family offices, corporates), then invest it over several years; the still-uninvested portion is dry powder. High dry powder signals future funding supply — funds earn by investing, not by sitting. But during slowdowns you will read ''record dry powder'' headlines even as cheques stall: having powder and pulling the trigger are different things.', 'Through India''s 2022-23 funding winter, India-focused funds reportedly sat on $15-20B of dry powder — reassurance that money would return once valuations corrected.', 'vc_speak', '{"Deal Flow","Micro VC","Seed Round"}'),
  ('Pro-Rata Rights', 'An investor''s right to invest again in future rounds to maintain their ownership percentage instead of getting diluted.', 'If an angel owns 5% after your seed round, pro-rata rights let them invest enough in the Series A to still own 5% after it. Investors prize this because it lets them double down on winners — most venture returns come from concentrating in breakouts. For founders it is usually harmless, but stacked pro-rata from many small investors can crowd out the space a new lead wants in a hot round.', 'An early Razorpay or Flipkart angel who kept exercising pro-rata through later rounds multiplied their eventual payout many times over versus quietly getting diluted.', 'legal_equity', '{"Dilution","Term Sheet","Cap Table"}'),
  ('Liquidation Preference', 'On exit, preferred investors take their money back first (usually 1x) before founders and employees split whatever remains.', 'A 1x non-participating liquidation preference — the Indian and global standard — means on a sale the investor takes either their invested amount back or their ownership share of proceeds, whichever is higher; everyone else splits the rest. It protects investor downside in mediocre exits and barely matters in great ones. Watch for aggressive variants in term sheets: 2x preference or ''participating'' preference (double-dipping) can leave founders with crumbs from a decent-looking sale.', 'A VC put ₹10 crore for 20%; the startup sells for ₹30 crore. Their 20% share is only ₹6 crore, so they instead take their ₹10 crore back first — founders and ESOP holders share the remaining ₹20 crore.', 'legal_equity', '{"Term Sheet","Equity","Down Round"}'),
  ('Non-Dilutive Funding', 'Money that costs you no equity: government grants, prizes, revenue, and (mostly) debt. Your ownership stays fully intact.', 'Non-dilutive capital funds the company without issuing shares. For Indian student founders the menu is rich: NIDHI PRAYAS prototype grants (up to ₹10 lakh), Startup India Seed Fund (up to ₹20 lakh grant plus ₹50 lakh convertible support), BIRAC''s BIG grant (₹50 lakh for biotech), state schemes, hackathon prizes — and the most underrated source, customer revenue. Judge it by strings attached: grants cost time and paperwork, debt must be repaid, but none of them touch your cap table.', 'An IIT Madras incubatee can stack a ₹10 lakh NIDHI PRAYAS grant with Startup India Seed Fund support and build for a year before pitching a single VC.', 'fundraising', '{"Dilution","Venture Debt","DPIIT Recognition","Accelerator vs Incubator"}'),
  ('Angel Investor', 'An individual investing their own money in very early startups — typically ₹5 lakh to ₹1 crore in India — often adding mentorship.', 'Angels write personal cheques at the riskiest stage, usually pre-seed and seed, betting on founders more than spreadsheets; many are founders or operators themselves who add intros and advice. In India they invest directly, via syndicates and platforms (AngelList India, LetsVenture, Mumbai Angels), or informally. Judge an angel by value beyond money — one respected operator-angel''s intro can outweigh a bigger silent cheque.', 'CRED''s Kunal Shah has angel-backed 200+ startups with early cheques; the Sharks on Shark Tank India are essentially angels investing personal money on national TV.', 'fundraising', '{"Pre-Seed","Micro VC","iSAFE"}'),
  ('Micro VC', 'A small VC fund (roughly ₹50-400 crore corpus) writing first cheques of ₹25 lakh to ₹3 crore at pre-seed and seed stage.', 'Micro VCs sit between angels and large funds: institutional discipline, a small corpus, early-stage focus, and speed — decisions in weeks, not quarters. Unlike angels they invest other people''s money, and they aim to get in before big funds price the deal up. India has 100+ of them; for a student founder they are often the first institutional ''yes'' and a bridge into larger funds'' deal flow.', 'Indian micro VCs and pre-seed funds like Titan Capital, First Cheque and Better Capital wrote some of the earliest cheques into Razorpay, Urban Company and Khatabook.', 'fundraising', '{"Angel Investor","Pre-Seed","Deal Flow"}'),
  ('Venture Debt', 'Loans for VC-backed startups — extends runway with minimal dilution. Comes with interest, repayment, and small equity warrants.', 'Venture debt lenders (in India: Trifecta, Alteria, Stride Ventures, InnoVen) lend to startups that already have VC backing, since the equity cushion de-risks the loan. Typical Indian terms: 13-16% interest, 2-3 year repayment, plus warrants (rights to buy a small slice of equity) as an upside kicker. It is fuel, not oxygen: great for extending runway between rounds or funding inventory, dangerous if used to postpone fixing broken unit economics — it must be repaid whether or not you grow.', 'After its equity Series B, an Indian D2C brand borrows ₹25 crore from Alteria to fund festive-season inventory — stretching runway while giving up well under 1% in warrants.', 'fundraising', '{"Runway","Bridge Round","Non-Dilutive Funding"}'),
  ('Accelerator vs Incubator', 'Accelerator: short cohort, small cheque for equity, ends at Demo Day. Incubator: longer-term space and mentorship, often equity-free.', 'Accelerators compress growth: you join a 3-6 month batch, get a standard investment (YC: $500K; 100X.VC: about ₹1.25 crore via iSAFE), intense mentorship, and pitch investors at Demo Day. Incubators — in India usually attached to institutes (SINE at IIT Bombay, IIT Madras Incubation Cell, NSRCEL at IIM Bangalore, T-Hub) — offer workspace, labs, mentors and grant access over 1-3 years, frequently without taking equity. Choose by stage: incubate to find your idea''s feet, accelerate to scale one that is already moving.', 'A student team might build inside IIT Madras Incubation Cell for a year (no equity taken), then join an accelerator like Y Combinator or Surge for money, network and a Demo Day stage.', 'fundraising', '{"Demo Day","Pre-Seed","Non-Dilutive Funding"}'),
  ('Demo Day', 'The graduation finale of an accelerator batch — each startup pitches for a few minutes to a room full of investors.', 'Demo Day compresses months of accelerator work into a 2-4 minute pitch — traction, market, team, ask — delivered to hundreds of angels and VCs at once. Its real product is FOMO and concentrated deal flow; strong startups walk out with packed calendars and term sheets. It is judged by follow-through, not applause: meetings booked and cheques closed in the following weeks are the real score.', 'Razorpay pitched at Y Combinator''s Winter 2015 Demo Day; in India, 100X.VC''s Pitch Day puts each iSAFE-funded class in front of hundreds of investors in Mumbai.', 'fundraising', '{"Accelerator vs Incubator","Deal Flow","Seed Round"}'),
  ('DPIIT Recognition', 'Official startup status from the Indian government (Startup India), unlocking tax holidays, easier compliance and seed-fund access.', 'The Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade recognises startups that are under 10 years old, have under ₹100 crore turnover, and work on innovation — you apply free on the Startup India portal with incorporation documents. Perks: an income-tax holiday for 3 of your first 10 years (Section 80-IAC, separate approval), self-certification under labour and environment laws, 80% patent-fee rebate with fast-tracking, easier public procurement and winding-up, plus eligibility for the Startup India Seed Fund and for issuing convertible notes. Over 1.5 lakh startups were recognised by 2025 — treat it as step one of Indian startup paperwork.', 'A two-founder Pune SaaS gets DPIIT recognition within days using just its incorporation docs and a writeup; that status later lets it raise ₹50 lakh via convertible notes and claim its 80-IAC tax holiday.', 'legal_equity', '{"Non-Dilutive Funding","Convertible Note","FEMA & FDI"}'),
  ('FEMA & FDI', 'FEMA is India''s law governing foreign money; FDI is foreign investment into your company. Foreign cheques bring RBI filings and rules.', 'When a foreign investor — a US VC, a Singapore fund — buys shares in your Indian private limited company, that is FDI (Foreign Direct Investment), regulated by FEMA (Foreign Exchange Management Act) through the RBI. The basics founders need: most sectors allow 100% FDI via the ''automatic route'' (no prior approval); shares must be issued at or above fair market value; and Form FC-GPR must be filed with the RBI within 30 days of allotting shares. One big exception: investors from countries sharing a land border with India (notably China) have needed prior government approval since 2020. Miss the filings and penalties surface at your next round''s due diligence.', 'When a Singapore-domiciled fund like Peak XV wires $2M into a Gurugram startup, the company files FC-GPR with RBI within 30 days; the post-2020 border-country rule is why Chinese funds behind early Paytm-era bets stopped writing new India cheques.', 'legal_equity', '{"DPIIT Recognition","Term Sheet","Due Diligence"}')
